WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.717
A Boolean column can have three meaningful states when NULL is allowed: true, false, and unknown.

00:00:07.717 --> 00:00:13.107
Consider a consent field. TRUE may mean the person explicitly agreed.

00:00:13.107 --> 00:00:16.392
FALSE may mean the person explicitly declined.

00:00:16.392 --> 00:00:20.197
NULL may mean nobody has recorded a decision yet.

00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:27.697
Those are three different business states, and treating NULL as if it were FALSE can destroy that distinction.

00:00:27.697 --> 00:00:32.732
PostgreSQL gives us predicates such as IS TRUE, IS FALSE, and IS UNKNOWN.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:36.767
These make the intention very clear.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:42.952
Imagine an approval workflow. approved IS TRUE means approval was explicitly granted.

00:00:42.952 --> 00:00:46.657
approved IS FALSE means it was explicitly rejected.

00:00:46.657 --> 00:00:51.177
approved IS UNKNOWN means a decision has not been recorded.

00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:56.682
That is much more informative than simply saying approved or NOT approved.

00:00:56.682 --> 00:01:01.197
Run the example and inspect the patient whose consent is NULL.

00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:07.812
The explicit-yes flag should be false, the explicit-no flag should also be false,

00:01:07.812 --> 00:01:10.757
and the not-recorded flag should be true.

00:01:10.757 --> 00:01:18.117
This is particularly important in domains where the difference between “no” and “we do not know” matters,

00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:24.822
such as privacy consent, legal approval, eligibility review, identity verification, or clinical confirmation.

00:01:24.822 --> 00:01:32.847
When a Boolean column is nullable, do not automatically think of it as a two-state switch.

00:01:32.847 --> 00:01:35.993
Ask what the third state means in the real process.
